{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The maximum likelihood estimator $\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ml}}$ is asymptotically unbiased, meaning $\\mathbb{E}[\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ml}}] \\to \\theta$ as $n \\to \\infty$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Timestamp [00:22:53\u201300:23:38]: 'This means that theta-ml is a random variable, right? And if it's random variable, we can ask ourselves what would be its expectation and what would be its variance, for example. And we can prove that its expectation is asymptotically unbiased. So it equals the true value of our unknown parameter theta when n is sufficiently large.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The maximum likelihood estimator $\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ml}}$ is consistent, meaning $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ml}} = \\theta$ almost surely.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Timestamp [00:24:25\u201300:25:07]: 'if the number of observations n goes to infinity, then we can easily prove, well, not easily, but we can prove, and this was done in statistics courses, that our maximum likelihood estimate converges to the true value when n goes to infinity.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "For large $n$, the maximum likelihood estimator $\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ml}}$ has an asymptotic normal distribution with mean $\\theta$ and variance $\\mathcal{I}(\\theta)^{-1}/n$, where $\\mathcal{I}(\\theta)$ is the Fisher information matrix.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Timestamp [00:25:07\u201300:25:41]: 'it has almost a normal distribution with the mean of course the true value this follows from the property number one and with variance that looks like this so this is n times so-called feature information matrix inverse in particular this means that the variance of our maximum value estimator goes to zero when n goes to plus infinity'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The maximum likelihood estimator $\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ml}}$ is asymptotically efficient, achieving the Cram\u00e9r-Rao lower bound for variance among all unbiased estimators.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Timestamp [00:26:23\u201300:27:28]: 'it appears that maximum likelihood estimate has the smallest possible variance. So it has exactly this lower bound for the variance. So this means that no other unbiased estimate can have smaller variance that maximum likelihood estimator has.'"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The ASR mentions 'Raul Karamer bound' (likely a mispronunciation of Cram\u00e9r-Rao bound) but the notes correctly identify it as the Cram\u00e9r-Rao bound. The ASR also states that the variance of the MLE goes to zero as $n \\to \\infty$ (consistent with consistency), but the notes correctly frame it as asymptotic normality with variance $\\mathcal{I}(\\theta)^{-1}/n$."
}
